`navigateWithQueryParams(\`/\${viewMode}/\${firstFiltered}\`)` uses the `viewMode` delivered by the `STORY_SPECIFIED` event (the preview iframe's current mode), not the type of the `firstFiltered` entry. If `firstFiltered` resolves to a docs entry (e.g., `type: 'docs'`) while the event carries `viewMode: 'story'`, the manager navigates to `/story/<docs-id>`, which is an invalid path and will fail to render the docs page. The navigation path should be derived from the target entry's own type:

```typescript
if (firstFiltered) {
  const targetType = filteredIndex![firstFiltered].type; // 'story' | 'docs'
  navigateWithQueryParams(`/${targetType}/${firstFiltered}`);
}
```

If the `'url'` persistence handler has not been registered yet (e.g., because the url-module's `init()` hasn't run) when the first `setState` with `persistence: 'url'` is called, the block silently does nothing — filter changes are neither reflected in the URL nor surfaced as an error. Add an `else` branch that logs a warning:

```typescript
} else {
  logger.warn('[Store] No URL persistence handler registered; filter state was not written to the URL.');
}
```
This makes timing / wiring bugs visible during development.

Tag values are interpolated directly into the URL string without any percent-encoding. A tag name containing `&`, `=`, `;`, or `!` would corrupt the URL structure: `&` would be parsed as a parameter delimiter, `=` as a key/value separator, and `;` as a tag-list delimiter. While Storybook tag names are typically simple identifiers, the encoder should guard against edge cases:

```typescript
const serializedIncluded = included.map(
  (tag) => encodeURIComponent(reverseBuiltInUrlTagMap[tag] ?? tag)
);
const serializedExcluded = excluded.map(
  (tag) => `!${encodeURIComponent(reverseBuiltInUrlTagMap[tag] ?? tag)}`
);
```
And `parseTagsParam` should correspondingly call `decodeURIComponent` after splitting.
